demo> /help output_level
NAME
  output_level - How loud commands are (/term.output).

VALUE
  output_level = "normal"
  Valid: silent, quiet, normal, verbose

FILE
  demo.cfg  "output_level": "normal"
  The active config, JSON, in its own folder.  /cfg.edit opens it in the editor (Ctrl+F finds a key); /help.open config explains the file.

USED BY
  /term.output  the value a load starts from

  A change by /cfg output_level <value> lasts the session; the config editor (/cfg.edit) saves it to demo.cfg.
demo> /help cycle
NAME
  cycle - Move a setting to its next value, wrapping at the end; made for a custom button.

DESCRIPTION
  /term.output cycle moves the output level to its next value, in the order the
  synopsis lists them, and the last value wraps back to the first.

  It is most useful on a custom button, where each click steps to the next value.
  Give the button this command (/help custom_buttons has the rest):

    "command": "/term.output cycle"

  A value sets the setting outright, and with no argument it only shows it.

TAKEN BY
  /term.eol, /term.eol.rx, /term.json, /term.output

SEE ALSO
  /help.idioms
demo> /help json
NAME
  json - Answer this one call as a JSON object, for a script or an agent.

DESCRIPTION
  Any command takes --json, and so does a line for the device. That one call
  answers as one JSON object: cmd, success, error, value, data, output_lines.

    /port.list --json
    AT+INFO --json

  /term.request on does the same for the whole session, and /term.json sets
  how the JSON prints. The flag is taken off the line wherever it appears, so a
  command's text cannot carry it. Typed as /help --json it means help as JSON,
  which is why this page is /help json.

SEE ALSO
  /help.idioms
demo> /help.idioms
  idioms - 6 idioms
  NAME     MEANING
  -- --
  boolean  A yes or no, in any of these words: on/off/true/false/yes/no/1/0/y/n/t/f.
  toggle   Flip a yes or no setting to its other value; made for a custom button.
  cycle    Move a setting to its next value, wrapping at the end; made for a custom button.
  bare     A setting with no argument shows its value and changes nothing.
  json     Answer this one call as a JSON object, for a script or an agent.
  level    How much one call prints, from nothing to all its detail.
demo> /help term.hex
NAME
  /term.hex - Toggle hex display of incoming bytes.

CURRENT STATE
  off

SYNOPSIS
  /term.hex {on|off|toggle}

DESCRIPTION
  Shows received bytes as hex rows: a row ends after each
  0A, or at 16 bytes. Rows are built from the bytes as they
  arrived, so CR, LF, backspace and bytes the encoding cannot
  decode all show as themselves, and a clear-screen escape is
  shown rather than obeyed.

  Display only: the session log, /expect and text
  captures still get the decoded lines, and sent text is not
  shown in hex. /proto.hex sets the same flag.

CONFIG
  hex = false  the value a load starts from
demo> /help term.hex --verbose
NAME
  /term.hex - Toggle hex display of incoming bytes.

STATUS
  stable - tested and part of the standard release

CURRENT STATE
  off

SYNOPSIS
  /term.hex {on|off|toggle}

DESCRIPTION
  Shows received bytes as hex rows: a row ends after each
  0A, or at 16 bytes. Rows are built from the bytes as they
  arrived, so CR, LF, backspace and bytes the encoding cannot
  decode all show as themselves, and a clear-screen escape is
  shown rather than obeyed.

  Display only: the session log, /expect and text
  captures still get the decoded lines, and sent text is not
  shown in hex. /proto.hex sets the same flag.

CONFIG
  hex = false  the value a load starts from
  A change by /cfg <key> <value> lasts the session; the config editor (/cfg.edit) saves it to demo.cfg.

AVAILABLE
  TUI: yes   CLI: yes (current)   MCP: yes

SEE ALSO
  /term.color, /term.control_keys, /term.ctrl, /term.echo, /term.echo_repl, /term.encoding, /term.eol, /term.info, /term.json, /term.line_endings, /term.line_no, /term.log, /term.output, /term.request, /term.send, /term.send_bare_enter, /term.timestamps, /term.usb_db, /term.verbose, /term
