demo> /help port
NAME
  /port - Serial port tools: connect, disconnect, list, configure.

SYNOPSIS
  /port {name}
  /port -- bare: opens the Port picker in the TUI; elsewhere runs /port.list

DESCRIPTION
  Connect to, disconnect from, list, or configure the serial port.
  Subcommands cover live signals (DTR/RTS/CTS/DSR/RI/CD), USB chip
  identification, and the mode settings.

  Connected: DEMO @ 115200 8N1

CONFIG
  serial.port = "DEMO"  set for the session, then reconnects

SUBCOMMANDS
  /port.baud_rate       Show or set baud rate.
  /port.break           Send a break signal (default 250ms).
  /port.byte_size       Show or set data bits.
  /port.cd              Show Carrier Detect state (read-only).
  /port.chip            Identify USB-serial chip(s) and report USB speed class.
  /port.connect         Connect to the serial port (e.g. /port.connect COM3 9600 N81).
  /port.cts             Show CTS state (read-only).
  /port.disconnect      Disconnect from the serial port.
  /port.dsr             Show DSR state (read-only).
  /port.dtr             Show or set DTR line (hardware only).
  /port.flow_control    Show or set flow control (none/rtscts/xonxoff/manual).
  /port.help            Show /port help.
  /port.info            Print status, params, chip, and live signals for the connected port.
  /port.list            List available serial ports.
  /port.mode            Show or set serial mode (e.g. /port.mode 9600 N81).
  /port.parity          Show or set parity.
  /port.ri              Show Ring Indicator state (read-only).
  /port.rts             Show or set RTS line (hardware only).
  /port.stop_bits       Show or set stop bits.
  /port.usb             Show the whole USB tree, with serial ports marked.
demo> /help cfg
NAME
  /cfg - Show or set config values.

SYNOPSIS
  /cfg {key {value}}
  /cfg -- bare: opens the Cfg picker in the TUI; elsewhere runs /cfg.list

DESCRIPTION
  A config is one device's settings: a JSON file in its own folder, with
  that device's scripts, logs and captures beside it. Create, load, edit
  and change configs here.

  Active cfg = demo (2 configs available)

  Three modes:
    /cfg                  - TUI: open Cfg picker.  CLI: list configs.
    /cfg baud_rate        - show current value of 'baud_rate'
    /cfg baud_rate 115200 - change it (the TUI asks first)

  The value is read as the key's current type (int, float, bool,
  string). Bool accepts:
    on/off/true/false/yes/no/1/0/y/n/t/f
  A key with a fixed set of values (parity, json_style,
  custom_buttons_side, ...) takes one of them in any case.
  A change lasts for this session; to keep it, set it in the
  config editor.

  Use /cfg.dump to print every key/value pair as JSON.
  Use /cfg.auto to set values without confirmation (for scripts).

SUBCOMMANDS
  /cfg.auto       Set immediately (no confirmation).
  /cfg.delete     Delete a config and its whole folder (asks first).
  /cfg.dump       Print the config in memory as JSON to the terminal.
  /cfg.edit       Edit the current config file.
  /cfg.explore    Open config directory in file explorer.
  /cfg.file       Print a file from the config folder to the terminal.
  /cfg.help       Show /cfg help.
  /cfg.icon       Create a desktop / menu launcher for the current cfg.
  /cfg.info       Print project summary (tree + config + buttons).
  /cfg.list       List all config files.
  /cfg.load       Load a different config in this session (hot-swap).
  /cfg.loaded     Show what the config load loaded: plugins, symbols, devices.
  /cfg.new        Create a config for a port; /cfg.load <name> switches to it.
  /cfg.rename     Rename a config, folder and file together.
  /cfg.show       Open the current config file in the system viewer.

CONFIGS
  termapy_cfg/ - 2 configs
  NAME SIZE UPDATED PORT BAUD TITLE
  -- -- -- -- -- --
  demo *  <SIZE>  just now  DEMO     115200  Demo Device
  gold_renamed  <SIZE>  just now  loop://    9600  gold_new
demo> /help var
NAME
  /var - List user variables, or show one by name.

SYNOPSIS
  /var {name}

DESCRIPTION
  Named values you set or capture from a command or a device reply, then
  use in any command as $(NAME).

  Currently defined: 0 user, 9 datetime, 15 context, 1 launch

  User-defined variables use $(NAME) syntax (case-sensitive).

  Setting variables (no / prefix needed):
    $(PORT) = COM7                - store literal value
    $(BAUD) <- /port.baud_rate    - capture REPL command output
    $(TEMP) <- AT+TEMP            - capture device response

  Using variables in commands:
    /print $(PORT)
    AT+PORT=$(PORT)

  Commands:
    /var                   - list all variables
    /var PORT              - show one variable (bare name or $(PORT))
    /var.list              - list all variables (alias of bare /var)
    /var.set PORT val      - set a variable (literal string)
    /var.capture NAME cmd  - run cmd and store its result as NAME
    /var.clear             - clear all variables

  Dynamic variables (current clock at point of use):
    $(DATE)              - current date (YYYY-MM-DD)
    $(TIME)              - current time (HH:MM:SS)
    $(DATETIME)          - current date and time

  Custom format (any datetime variable, strftime after a colon):
    $(DATETIME:%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)  - filename-safe stamp (colon-free)
    $(TIME:%H%M)               - hour+minute only
    $(SESSION_DATE:%d-%b)      - works on frozen vars too

  Launch variables (frozen when the app starts):
    $(LAUNCH_DATE)       - app start date
    $(LAUNCH_TIME)       - app start time
    $(LAUNCH_DATETIME)   - app start date and time

  Session variables (set once per script launch, frozen):
    $(SESSION_DATE)      - script start date
    $(SESSION_TIME)      - script start time
    $(SESSION_DATETIME)  - script start date and time

  Config variables (resolved paths from current config):
    $(CFG)               - config name (e.g. demo)
    $(CFG.DIR)           - config directory (resolved)
    $(CFG.FILE)          - config file path (resolved)
    $(CFG.LOG_FILE)      - log file path (resolved)
    $(CFG.RUN_DIR)       - scripts directory
    $(CFG.PROTO_DIR)     - protocol test directory
    $(CFG.PLUGIN_DIR)    - plugin directory
    $(CFG.SS_DIR)        - screenshot directory
    $(CFG.CAP_DIR)       - capture directory
    $(CFG.PROF_DIR)      - profile directory
    $(CFG.VIZ_DIR)       - visualizer directory
    $(CFG.PORT)          - serial port name
    $(CFG.BAUD)          - baud rate
    $(CFG.PORT_CFG)      - port config (e.g. 9600 8N1)
    $(CFG.PORT_FULL)     - full connection (e.g. COM4 9600 8N1)

  Escaping (when your data contains literal $):
    \$(PORT)           - literal $(PORT) (not expanded)
    /raw $(GPS),NMEA,0 - send entire line verbatim (no expansion)

  Scope: variables persist until a config loads, which starts clean.
  A script is not a load: it sees them however it was launched, and what
  it sets stays after it ends.
  Use /var.clear to reset manually.

SUBCOMMANDS
  /var.capture    Capture command output into a user variable.
  /var.clear      Clear all user variables.
  /var.list       Explicit alias for bare /var.
  /var.set        Set a user variable to a literal value.
